---
import CaseLayout from '../../components/CaseLayout.astro';
import Embed from '../../components/Embed.astro';
import Loop from '../../components/Loop.astro';
---
<CaseLayout
  title="Tava Health"
  description="An unsolicited concept for the moment people quietly stop going to therapy — a post-session check-in that turns a silent exit into a conversation."
  label="Unsolicited Concept // Product Design"
  cover="/assets/projects/tava/cover.jpg"
  coverAlt="Case Study: Tava Health — session recap, what felt off, and the options offered"
  meta={[
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Solo, unsolicited concept' },
    { label: 'Scope', value: 'Research · Flows · UI · Motion' },
    { label: 'Tools', value: 'Whimsical · Claude Design' },
    { label: 'Status', value: 'Concept, not client work' },
  ]}
  action={{ label: 'Watch the 5-minute walkthrough', href: 'https://cleanshot.com/share/NnpG0hZS' }}
>
  <p>
    Tava lets you switch therapists quickly, and it does that part well. The problem is the
    people who never get that far &mdash; one or two sessions in, no rebooking, then silence.
    They don&rsquo;t complain and they don&rsquo;t switch. They just stop. This is a concept
    for the moment that catches them: <strong>a post-session check-in that turns a quiet exit
    into a conversation.</strong>
  </p>

  <section class="case-gallery" slot="gallery">
    <div class="container project-content">
      <h2>The two ways people <span class="accent">leave</span>.</h2>
      <p>
        Two failure modes, and only one of them is visible.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first is switching. It works &mdash; but every switch costs a first session. You
        restart your history with someone new, and the intake burns the hour you came for.
        That came up constantly in research as the biggest complaint about changing providers.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second is the one I wanted to solve: <strong>people who just ghost.</strong>{' '}
        They&rsquo;re the majority, and the product never hears from them. The question
        isn&rsquo;t &ldquo;why did they leave?&rdquo; It&rsquo;s <em>why didn&rsquo;t they
        rebook?</em>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="container">
      <Embed
        src="https://whimsical.com/embed/6nyXiBadHgYp5eWFwiDqwh"
        title="Tava research board"
        label="Open the research board"
        note="Whimsical — start at the green marker, top left"
        aspect="16 / 11"
        openHref="https://whimsical.com/hunter-s-workspace84/tava-6nyXiBadHgYp5eWFwiDqwh"
        openLabel="Open the board in Whimsical"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="container project-content">
      <h2>Eleven directions, then <span class="accent">two</span>.</h2>
      <p>
        I started in Whimsical with sketches and wireframes &mdash; about eleven directions for
        where an intervention could live. I compared all of them against the same four
        questions: <strong>when</strong> do you ask, <strong>what</strong> do you ask for,{' '}
        <strong>how</strong> is it framed, and <strong>who</strong> starts it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two survived. A <strong>concierge chat</strong>, where a person or an AI sits between
        you and the problem and surfaces prebuilt cards &mdash; here&rsquo;s a fix, here are
        people you could try, here are open times. And a <strong>post-session check-in</strong>,
        which is simpler and lands while the feeling is still fresh.
      </p>
      <p>
        I flowcharted both and compared them side by side. The check-in ran bigger as a flow,
        and it&rsquo;s the one I picked.
      </p>

    </div>

    <div class="container">
      <Loop
        src="/assets/projects/tava/motion/check-in.mp4"
        poster="/assets/projects/tava/motion/check-in.jpg"
        alt="The post-session check-in: session recap, then what felt off, then sent"
        caption="The check-in, end to end: recap &rarr; what felt off &rarr; sent."
      />
    </div>

    <div class="container project-content">
      <h2>The part I&rsquo;m <span class="accent">proud</span> of.</h2>
      <p>
        Most of the time, leaving isn&rsquo;t about the therapist being wrong. It&rsquo;s about
        one thing that never got said.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the check-in offers to say it for you. <em>&ldquo;What would you like them to
        know?&rdquo;</em> &mdash; a few starter prompts, or write your own. Then, before
        anything sends: <strong>&ldquo;Here&rsquo;s exactly what we&rsquo;ll send.&rdquo;</strong>{' '}
        You see the message, you can edit it, you can back out.
      </p>
      <div class="media-split">
        <div class="media-split__media">
          <Loop
            variant="tall"
            src="/assets/projects/tava/motion/review-send.mp4"
            poster="/assets/projects/tava/motion/review-send.jpg"
            alt="Review and send: the exact message, who it comes from, and when it arrives"
          />
        </div>
        <div class="media-split__text">
          <p>
            <strong>It isn&rsquo;t anonymous, on purpose.</strong> If a provider has one
            client, anonymity is a promise you can&rsquo;t keep &mdash; so don&rsquo;t make it.
            What Tava can offer instead is mediation: it comes from Tava, on your behalf, and
            there&rsquo;s a paper trail.
          </p>
          <p>
            The three lines under the message are doing the real work &mdash;{' '}
            <em>they&rsquo;ll get it before Friday, she&rsquo;ll know it&rsquo;s from you, you
            don&rsquo;t have to bring it up.</em> Every one answers a reason somebody would
            close the screen instead of sending.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>And if you do want <span class="accent">someone new</span>.</h2>
      <p>
        The switch path attacks the thing that makes switching expensive: starting over. You
        pick from a short list of providers, then choose what goes with you &mdash; past notes,
        AI summaries, assessments. Your next therapist starts already knowing something about
        you, so session one isn&rsquo;t spent rehashing. Then date, time, summary, and
        it&rsquo;s on your calendar.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="container">
      <Loop
        src="/assets/projects/tava/motion/switch-book.mp4"
        poster="/assets/projects/tava/motion/switch-book.jpg"
        alt="Switching: choose a provider, choose what to share, book the first session"
        caption="Switching without starting over: pick someone, choose what goes with you, book."
      />
    </div>

    <div class="container">
      <Embed
        src="/prototypes/tava/index.html"
        title="Tava post-session flow prototype"
        label="Open the live prototype"
        note="Interactive — includes the motion studies"
        poster="/assets/projects/tava/cover.jpg"
        aspect="16 / 10"
        openHref="/prototypes/tava/index.html"
        openLabel="Open the prototype full screen"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="container project-content">
      <div class="media-split media-split--flip">
        <div class="media-split__text">
          <p>
            Booking is where good intentions go to die, so it stays inside the same sheet
            &mdash; month, day, time, confirm. No hand-off to a separate scheduler, no
            second app, no losing the thread you just worked up the nerve to pull.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="media-split__media">
          <Loop
            variant="tall"
            src="/assets/projects/tava/motion/date-picker.mp4"
            poster="/assets/projects/tava/motion/date-picker.jpg"
            alt="Choosing a date and time inside the same sheet"
          />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Motion, because it&rsquo;s the <span class="accent">whole question</span>.</h2>
      <p>
        Calm, functional motion &mdash; no celebration. Nothing cheers when you tell your
        therapist something isn&rsquo;t working. Whether a flow like this feels gentle or
        clinical can&rsquo;t be answered in a static frame, so every transition is a replayable
        study in the prototype above.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>
